Remember our cheat sheet:

LL → RIGHT
RR → LEFT

LR → LEFT + RIGHT
RL → RIGHT + LEFT

We actually only need to write two rotation functions.

Because LR and RL are combinations of these two.

Right rotation

Imagine:

      y
     /
    x
   /
  T1

Right rotation around y gives:

      x
     / \
   T1   y

Code:

struct Node* rightRotate(struct Node *y)
{
    struct Node *x = y->left;
    struct Node *T2 = x->right;

    x->right = y;
    y->left = T2;

    return x;
}
Just understand these three lines:

x = y->left;
T2 = x->right;

Then:

x->right = y;
y->left = T2;

Left rotation

Opposite:

  x
   \
    y
     \
      T2

becomes:

      y
     / \
    x   T2

Code:

struct Node* leftRotate(struct Node *x)
{
    struct Node *y = x->right;
    struct Node *T2 = y->left;

    y->left = x;
    x->right = T2;

    return y;
}

See the symmetry?
RIGHT ROTATE              LEFT ROTATE

x = y->left               y = x->right
T2 = x->right             T2 = y->left

x->right = y              y->left = x
y->left = T2              x->right = T2


Balance factor
Now:

int getBalance(struct Node *node)
{
    if (node == NULL)
        return 0;

    return height(node->left) - height(node->right);
}

So:

BF = LEFT HEIGHT - RIGHT HEIGHT

For example:

      12
     /
    8
   /
  5
At 12:

left height  = 2
right height = 0

BF = 2 - 0
   = +2

Unbalanced.
